ifferential tests\n- It likely doesn't depend on pkg/sys or pkg/format\n\nAnd pkg/format:\n- Provides formatting for cmd/ packages\n- Might be used by several utilities (ls, du)\n- Doesn't depend on other pkg/ packages\n\nAnd pkg/sys:\n- Provides syscall abstractions\n- Used by cmd/ packages\n- pkg/format might use terminal size from pkg/sys (for column width)\n\nNow I'm thinking through the actual dependencies more carefully. pkg/sys handles terminal size queries while pkg/format handles column alignment for tabular output. If pkg/format needs to know terminal width for proper alignment, then pkg/sys would need to come first. But for an initial implementation, pkg/format could just format strings without terminal awareness—the caller could handle terminal size separately. So the question is whether to start with pkg/sys as a foundational dependency or pkg/format as a more immediately useful utility.\n\nGiven the alphabetical tiebreaker rule, pkg/format comes first.\n\nNow I need to figure out the file structure. The naming convention says primary source files should be named after the primary exported types, not the package name. For pkg/format, that means I'm looking at types like a column formatter for tabular alignment, a color scheme handler for ANSI codes, and something for human-readable size formatting. Let me think through what those primary types should actually be called.\n\nThe problem is I'm inventing type names without seeing the PRD, which violates the constraint about not making design decisions outside the architecture. The architecture describes capabilities but not the specific types. And I'm supposed to anchor tasks to specific requirement IDs, which I can't do without the actual PRD contents.\n\nI think the pragmatic solution here is to reference the PRD directly in my task description and let the stitch agent work from there, rather than guessing at type names that might not match what's actually specified. and use the architecture's capability